before:
```sql
CREATE TABLE plans(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT);
INSERT INTO plans VALUES (1,'Map'),(2,'Sketch');
BEGIN;
ALTER TABLE plans ADD COLUMN status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'draft';
ALTER TABLE plans RENAME COLUMN title TO label;
COMMIT;
SELECT id,label,status
FROM plans
ORDER BY id;
```
The result is (1,'Map','draft'),(2,'Sketch','draft'). Existing rows read the new default. NOT NULL rejects missing status values, and DEFAULT supplies a value when it is omitted. SQLite 3.49.1 ADD COLUMN has restrictions: on this populated table, adding a NOT NULL column without a non-NULL default fails. Newer ALTER COLUMN features are not taught here.

```sql
CREATE TABLE plans(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT);
INSERT INTO plans VALUES (1,'Map'),(2,'Sketch');
BEGIN;
ALTER TABLE plans ADD COLUMN status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'draft';
ALTER TABLE plans RENAME COLUMN title TO label;
ROLLBACK;
SELECT id,title
FROM plans
ORDER BY id;
```
ROLLBACK restores the original title column and removes status. The final query returns (1,Map),(2,Sketch). A failed statement is not an automatic substitute for rolling back a whole multi-statement migration. The supplemental failed-add fixture checks its diagnostic and preserved schema; the learner’s SELECT checks do not grade intentional errors.

